  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Findings: reach of the localized P-bilinears (adjoint, exact, points 1 and 2 identical)\n\nChecks: $t_c=\\Pi(P)t_d\\Pi(P)^{-1}$ holds, and after $\\Pi(P)$ and $g_0^{-1}$ every state is still a product with site 2 exactly $\\propto e_1^T$.\n\n| localized state (companion) | singlet | octets (mult 2) | (3,3,0) | (4,1,1) | (4,2,0) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| $e_5\\otimes e_8$: $(1,2,0,1)|_{v=\\theta_1}$, $(2,2,0,2)|_{u=\\theta_1}$ | **0** | rank 1 | 0 | ✓ | ✓ |\n| $e_7\\otimes e_8$: $(2,2,1,2)|_{u=\\theta_1+2h}$ | **0** | 0 | ✓ | 0 | ✓ |\n| $e_3\\otimes e_8$: $(2,2,0,1)|_{(\\theta_1+2h,\\theta_1)}$ | **0** | rank 1 | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| control: $e_1\\otimes e_8$ | ≠ 0 | rank 1 | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |\n\n**No new singlet.** The reason is exact. The singlet is invariant under global $g\\otimes g$, so the frame change and the undo of $g_0$ cannot create a singlet overlap. That overlap equals the companion-frame one, and by weight $e_k^T\\otimes e_8^T$ pairs with the singlet only for $e_k=e_1$ (weight $(2,1,0)$). All localized bilinears land on $e_3,e_5,e_7$, never on $e_1$. So on $e_8\\otimes e_8$ the question reduces to whether a localized covector has an $e_1$ component; these do not. They also reach nothing beyond the earlier families' sectors.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  }
